  "live_records": {
    "q-95709": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95709",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that an inbox with $100$ emails contains $45$ spam emails, given that the probability that a single email is spam is $0.47.$\n\nII. The probability that less than $2$ light bulbs are defective from a box containing $20$ lightbulbs if the probability of a single bulb being defective is $0.12.$\n\nIII. The probability of finding more than $16$ people with blood type A$-$ in a randomly selected sample of $50$ people, given that the probability that a randomly selected person has blood type A$-$ is $0.08.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.47.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.47 \\\\  & =47 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.47) \\\\  & =53 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=20$ and $p=0.12.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20\\cdot 0.12 \\\\  & =2.4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=50$ and $p=0.08.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.08 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
