    "q-94819": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94819",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a fair coin lands on heads $40$ times out of $100$ coin flips.\n\nII. The probability of getting $10$ ones on $20$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\nIII. The probability of a student getting $4$ correct answers on a true/false quiz with $5$ questions if the student answers all questions at random.",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=100$ and $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=20$ and $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=5$ and $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
