      "kp_id": "e-8285"
    },
    "q-132264": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132264",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of getting $10$ ones on $25$ rolls of a biased $6$-sided die if the probability of getting one in a single roll is $0.4.$\n\nII. The probability of a student getting more than $10$ correct answers on a $20$-question test if the student has $60%$ probability of correctly answering each question.\n\nIII. The probability that a fair spinner with twenty-five regions, numbered from $1$ through $25,$ lands on seven more than $5$ times out of $100$ spins.",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.4) \\\\  & =15 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=20$ and $p=0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =12 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=100$ and $p=\\frac{1}{25}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot \\frac{1}{25} \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
