    "q-132263": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132263",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of a student getting $9$ correct answers on a test with $12$ questions if each question has $3$ options and the student answers all questions randomly.\n\nII. The probability of finding more than $50$ people with blue eyes in a randomly selected sample of $250$ if $40%$ of the population has blue eyes.\n\nIII. The probability that a biased coin lands on tails $35$ times out of $50$ coin flips if the probability that a single flip landing on tails is $0.6.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=12$ and $p=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=250$ and $p=0.4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =250\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =100 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =250\\cdot (1-0.4) \\\\  & =150 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=50$ and $p=0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
