    "q-132272": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132272",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼B(110,0.5),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=65).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9641\\,$ | $\\,0.9649\\,$ | $\\,0.9656\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9773\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=110$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =110\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =55 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =110\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =55 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(110)(0.5)=55\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(110)(0.5)(1-0.5)=27.5.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(55,27.5).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=65).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=65)≈P(64.5\\le Y\\le 65.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(64.5\\le Y\\le 65.5) & =P(\\frac{64.5-55}{\\sqrt{27.5}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{65.5-55}{\\sqrt{27.5}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.81\\le Z\\le 2.00) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.00)-P(Z<1.81) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.00)-P(Z\\le 1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(2.00)-Φ(1.81) \\\\  & =0.9773-0.9649 \\\\  & =0.012\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=65)≈0.012.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0124.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
