    "q-95745": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95745",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that $35$ out of $90$ rolls of a $6$-sided die land on an even number.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0139\\,$ | $\\,0.0136\\,$ | $\\,0.0132\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0179\\,$ | $\\,0.0174\\,$ | $\\,0.0170\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0228\\,$ | $\\,0.0222\\,$ | $\\,0.0217\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=90$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =90\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =45 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =90\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =45 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(90)(0.5)=45\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(90)(0.5)(1-0.5)=22.5.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(45,22.5).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=35).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=35)≈P(34.5\\le Y\\le 35.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(34.5\\le Y\\le 35.5) & =P(\\frac{34.5-45}{\\sqrt{22.5}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{35.5-45}{\\sqrt{22.5}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.21\\le Z\\le -2.00) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -2.00)-P(Z<-2.21) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -2.00)-P(Z\\le -2.21) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.00)-Φ(-2.21) \\\\  & =0.0228-0.0136 \\\\  & =0.0092\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=35)≈0.009.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0092.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
