      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼B(210,0.6),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(120<X\\le 140).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1949\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1922\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1894\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2236\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2206\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2177\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2483\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9732\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9738\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9744\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,2.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9788\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9793\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9798\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,2.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9834\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9838\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9842\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=210$ and $p=0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =210\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =126 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =210\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =84 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(210)(0.6)=126\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(210)(0.6)(1-0.6)=50.4.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(126,50.4).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(120<X\\le 140).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(120<X\\le 140)=P(121\\le X\\le 140)≈P(120.5\\le Y\\le 140.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(120.5\\le Y\\le 140.5) & =P(\\frac{120.5-126}{\\sqrt{50.4}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{140.5-126}{\\sqrt{50.4}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.77\\le Z\\le 2.04) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.04)-P(Z<-0.77) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.04)-P(Z\\le -0.77) \\\\  & =Φ(2.04)-Φ(-0.77) \\\\  & =0.9793-0.2206 \\\\  & =0.7587\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(120<X\\le 140)≈0.759.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.7602.$",
