    "q-95812": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95812",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼B(120,0.41),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X<48).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3446\\,$ | $\\,0.3409\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3821\\,$ | $\\,0.3783\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=120$ and $p=0.41.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =120\\cdot 0.41 \\\\  & =49.2 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =120\\cdot (1-0.41) \\\\  & =70.8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(120)(0.41)=49.2\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(120)(0.41)(1-0.41)=29.028.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(49.2,29.028).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X<48).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X<48)=P(X\\le 47)≈P(Y\\le 47.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 47.5) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{47.5-49.2}{\\sqrt{29.028}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -0.32) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.32) \\\\  & =0.3745\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<48)≈0.375.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.3762.$",
