    "q-132403": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132403",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼B(160,0.55),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X\\le 95).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ | $\\,0.8599\\,$ | $\\,0.8621\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=160$ and $p=0.55.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =160\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =88 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =160\\cdot (1-0.55) \\\\  & =72 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(160)(0.55)=88\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(160)(0.55)(1-0.55)=39.6.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(88,39.6).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\le 95).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 95)≈P(Y\\le 95.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 95.5) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{95.5-88}{\\sqrt{39.6}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 1.19) \\\\  & =Φ(1.19) \\\\  & =0.883\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 95)≈0.883.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.8836.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
