    "q-95756": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95756",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "In an airport, $44%$ of the flights arrive early. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that if $120$ flights are picked at random, then more than $49$ but less than $70$ will arrive early. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2420\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2389\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2358\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2743\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2709\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3085\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,2.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9985\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9986\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9986\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,3.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9989\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9990\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9990\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,3.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9992\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9993\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9993\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=120$ and $p=0.44.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =120\\cdot 0.44 \\\\  & =52.8 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =120\\cdot (1-0.44) \\\\  & =67.2 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(120)(0.44)=52.8\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(120)(0.44)(1-0.44)=29.568.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(52.8,29.568).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(49<X<70).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(49<X<70)=P(50\\le X\\le 69)≈P(49.5\\le Y\\le 69.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.5\\le Y\\le 69.5) & =P(\\frac{49.5-52.8}{\\sqrt{29.568}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{69.5-52.8}{\\sqrt{29.568}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.61\\le Z\\le 3.07) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.07)-P(Z<-0.61) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.07)-P(Z\\le -0.61) \\\\  & =Φ(3.07)-Φ(-0.61) \\\\  & =0.9989-0.2709 \\\\  & =0.7280\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(49<X<70)≈0.728.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.7270.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
